WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.006
Password hashing is different from encryption.

2
00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:06.231
Encryption is reversible when the key is available.

3
00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:10.703
Password storage should not require recovering the original password.

4
00:00:10.703 --> 00:00:13.635
Instead, the system stores a one-way verifier.

5
00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:25.878
During login, the presented password is processed through the same password hashing scheme, and the result is compared to the stored verifier using the algorithm's expected verification routine.

6
00:00:25.878 --> 00:00:28.443
Salts are a core part of password hashing.

7
00:00:28.443 --> 00:00:33.135
A salt is a unique value generated for each password and stored with the hash.

8
00:00:33.135 --> 00:00:41.199
It ensures that two users with the same password do not have the same stored verifier, and it frustrates precomputed lookup tables.

9
00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:42.519
Salts are not secret.

10
00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:45.671
Their value comes from uniqueness and correct use.

11
00:00:45.671 --> 00:00:49.923
A system that uses the same salt everywhere loses most of the benefit.

12
00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:52.268
Work factors are another key concept.

13
00:00:52.268 --> 00:00:58.939
Password hashing should be deliberately expensive so that offline guessing is costly if hashes are stolen.

14
00:00:58.939 --> 00:01:05.097
Fast general-purpose hashes are not appropriate because attackers can test guesses very quickly.

15
00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:10.962
Purpose-built password hashing algorithms let teams tune cost over time as hardware changes.

16
00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:17.780
That tuning must be practical enough for normal authentication while expensive enough to slow bulk guessing.

17
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.445
Password hashes should still be treated as sensitive data.

18
00:01:21.445 --> 00:01:26.577
A hash is not the plaintext password, but it is a target for offline attack if stolen.

19
00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:35.813
Database access, backups, analytics exports, support tools, and incident response workflows should treat stored credential material carefully.

20
00:01:35.813 --> 00:01:37.793
Strong hashing reduces damage.

21
00:01:37.793 --> 00:01:41.825
It does not make the credential database safe to expose casually.
